            <div xml:id="d630">

                <pb facs="0647" n="547" sameAs="frus:frus1920v03/pg547"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.00/6672: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The President of the Technical Board</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Stevens</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Harbin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-03-29"><hi rendition="#italic">March 29,
                        1920</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received April 2—5:15 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>[From Smith:]</p>
                <p>March 29, 6 p.m. Chita. Colonel Blunt<anchor n="74" xml:id="d630-f1"/> arrived at
                    Chita to-day. I read to him my past telegrams about the Reds. He fully concurs
                    and adds following: Reds treated them very well. All illiterate under 35 years
                    must attend school 2 hours daily, upon taking town inhabitants must clean
                    streets and railways, women work 1 hour to show willingness, towns clean, no one
                    allowed to live in cars, food situation improving everywhere, nullification of
                    money caused some trouble soon adjusted, none of the reports about peasants
                    fighting Reds true. Reds anxious to make working agreement with America and to
                    obtain supplies, also that America send some one to study conditions in Russia.
                    Blunt says leaders honest, industrious, businesslike in their methods and have
                    confidence of all classes except speculators. … Smith.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stevens</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d630-f1" n="74" xml:id="d630-f1-content">
                <p>Of the Russian Railway Service Corps, detained by the Bolsheviks west of Irkutsk;
                    see telegram of Jan. 20 from the Consul at Harbin, <ref target="#pg530">p.
                        530</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
